-- title:
--   Uniqueness of splitting fields up to isomorphism
-- statement:
--   **Uniqueness of splitting fields.** Let $K$ be a field and $f\in K[X]$. If $L$ and $M$ are splitting fields of $f$ over $K$, then there is an isomorphism $L\cong M$ that is the identity on $K$.
--
--   A splitting field of $f$ is an extension in which $f$ factors into linear factors and which is generated by the roots of $f$. Because it is unique up to isomorphism, one can speak of the Galois group of a polynomial. The same result gives the uniqueness of the finite field $\mathbb F_{p^n}$ as the splitting field of $X^{p^n}-X$ over $\mathbb F_p$.
--
--   **Formalization note.** Mathlib's `Polynomial.IsSplittingField.algEquiv`, which gives an isomorphism from any splitting field to Mathlib's constructed splitting field `f.SplittingField`. The proof composes the isomorphism for $L$ with the inverse of the one for $M$. `IsSplittingField K L f` says that $f$ splits in $L$ and that $L$ is generated over $K$ by its roots.
-- source:
--   Listed in Mathlib's curated theorem manifests (docs/1000.yaml); formalized in Mathlib as `Polynomial.IsSplittingField.algEquiv`. Proof here reduces to that Mathlib result.

import Mathlib

namespace FamousTheorems

theorem splitting_field_unique_7b {K : Type*} (L M : Type*) [Field K] [Field L] [Field M] [Algebra K L] [Algebra K M]
    (f : Polynomial K) [Polynomial.IsSplittingField K L f] [Polynomial.IsSplittingField K M f] :
    Nonempty (L ≃ₐ[K] M) := by sorry

end FamousTheorems
